    <md:title>1.5.5: Examining an Equation in Two Variables and Its Graph, Part 2</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8888f0b0-1953-4f05-b70f-644bf7c85c7e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>A ceramic sugar bowl weighs 340 grams when empty. It is then filled with sugar. One tablespoon of sugar weighs 12.5 grams.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write an equation to represent the relationship between the total weight of the
      bowl in grams, \( W \), and the tablespoons of sugar, \( T \).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( W=340 + 12.5T \), or \( 340 + 12.5T = 740 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>When the sugar bowl is full, it weighs 740 grams. How many tablespoons of sugar can the bowl hold? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> </para>
<para>32 tablespoons;</para>

  <para>The graph represents the relationship between the number of tablespoons of sugar in the bowl and the total weight of the bowl.</para>

<figure><media alt="Graph of a line. Weight, grams. Sugar, tablespoons."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b1dadb12d00e370697db2a2a2d30229cbe3eab98"/></media></figure><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
<item>Which point on the graph could represent your answer to the previous question?</item></list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>  Point D, because its coordinates could be (32, 740).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>About how many tablespoons of sugar are in the bowl when the total weight is 600 grams?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> About 21 tablespoons. Looking at the graph, when the weight is 600, the line is about at 21.</para></section>
